# rules: kotlin-credential-in-shared-preferences
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-312.kt
# from: CWE-312 (Cleartext Storage of Sensitive Information), CWE-522
#
# SharedPreferences is an XML file in the app's data directory holding exactly
# what was written to it. That is private on an ordinary phone and it is not
# private on a rooted one, in a full-device backup, on any build with
# `android:allowBackup="true"` - the default - or to anything that gets code
# running as the app. A password kept there is a password that anyone with one
# copy of the device's data has, and unlike a session token it belongs to the
# user and is probably not only for this app.
#
# The key is the evidence, and it is better evidence than the value. A key is a
# literal at the call, so the rule can read it without following anything, and
# it is what the programmer decided this slot was for. `putString("password",
# whatever)` is a password slot however the value got there, which is the
# question - and reading the value instead would need a taint path from a login
# form that most apps do not write in one method.
#
# `putBoolean` and the rest are not sinks. A flag is not a credential whatever
# it is called, and the string is where the secret goes.
#
# The fix is EncryptedSharedPreferences from androidx.security, or better, a
# token in the keystore instead of the password at all. Neither is visible to
# this rule, so an app that has already moved to EncryptedSharedPreferences
# will still be reported: it is the same call on a different object, and the
# object's type is the one thing the syntax does not say.

["$E.putString($K, $V)", "$P.edit().putString($K, $V)"] as $stores
| "(?i)^\"[^\"]*(?:passw|secret|refresh[_-]?token|access[_-]?token|api[_-]?key|credential|private[_-]?key|passphrase|pin_?code)" as $namesASecret
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $stores) as $all
| ($all | of($stores) | where_capture("K"; $namesASecret) | focus("K"))
| finding("kotlin-credential-in-shared-preferences";
    "$K is written to a preferences file as it stands, which survives a backup "
    + "and is readable on a rooted phone; EncryptedSharedPreferences instead")
| report
